  "introTipsHtml": "final consonants are usually silent (<em>parlent</em> = &quot;parl&quot;). <strong>on</strong> (&quot;we/one&quot;) always uses the <em>il/elle</em> form.",
  "grammarNotes": [
    {
      "html": "<strong>Regular -er verbs (1st group)</strong> (rester, regarder, travailler, habiter...): drop <em>-er</em> and add <em>-e, -es, -e, -ons, -ez, -ent</em>. Past participle <em>-é</em> (<em>j&#39;ai regardé</em>), future = infinitive + <em>-ai</em> (<em>je regarderai</em>)."
    },
    {
      "html": "<strong>Regular -ir verbs (2nd group)</strong> (finir, choisir, réussir, réfléchir): drop <em>-ir</em> and add <em>-is, -is, -it, -issons, -issez, -issent</em>. The <em>-iss-</em> appears in the plural (<em>nous finissons</em>). Past participle <em>-i</em> (<em>j&#39;ai fini</em>)."
    },
    {
      "html": "<strong>Regular -re verbs</strong> (entendre, répondre, rendre, attendre, perdre, descendre, vendre): drop <em>-re</em> and add <em>-s, -s, —, -ons, -ez, -ent</em> (<em>il attend</em> has no ending). Past participle <em>-u</em> (<em>j&#39;ai attendu</em>); future drops the final <em>e</em> (<em>j&#39;attendrai</em>)."
    },
    {
      "html": "<strong>Spelling-change -er verbs</strong> keep regular endings but adjust the stem to keep the sound:<ul><li><em>c → ç</em> and <em>g → ge</em> before <em>o</em>: <em>nous commençons</em>, <em>nous changeons</em>, <em>nous voyageons</em>.</li><li>Double consonant: <em>j&#39;appelle / nous appelons</em>, <em>je jette / nous jetons</em>; future <em>j&#39;appellerai</em>, <em>je jetterai</em>.</li><li><em>e → è</em>: <em>j&#39;achète, je me lève, je me promène</em> (but <em>nous achetons</em>); future <em>j&#39;achèterai</em>.</li><li><em>é → è</em>: <em>je préfère, j&#39;espère, je répète</em> (but <em>nous préférons</em>); the future keeps <em>é</em> (<em>je préférerai</em>).</li><li><em>y → i</em> (optional for -ayer): <em>j&#39;essaie / j&#39;essaye</em>, <em>je paie / je paye</em>.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "html": "<strong>Irregular families</strong> — learn one, and you know the rest:<ul><li><strong>partir / sortir / dormir / sentir / servir</strong>: singular drops the last stem consonant (<em>je dors, je sors, je sens, je sers</em>), plural keeps it (<em>nous dormons, nous sortons</em>).</li><li><strong>venir / tenir / devenir / revenir / se souvenir</strong>: <em>je viens, je tiens ... nous venons, ils viennent</em>; past <em>venu / tenu</em>; future with <em>-dr-</em> (<em>je viendrai, je tiendrai, je me souviendrai</em>).</li><li><strong>prendre family</strong> (prendre, apprendre, comprendre): <em>j&#39;apprends, nous apprenons, ils apprennent</em>; past <em>appris</em>.</li><li><strong>mettre family</strong> (mettre, permettre, promettre): <em>je promets, nous promettons</em>; past <em>promis, permis</em>.</li><li><strong>ouvrir / offrir / découvrir</strong>: present uses -er endings (<em>j&#39;ouvre, tu offres, il découvre</em>); past <em>ouvert, offert, découvert</em>.</li><li>Others to learn individually: falloir, pleuvoir (impersonal, <em>il</em> only), vivre, écrire, suivre, lire, recevoir, reconnaître, courir, boire, mourir, envoyer, conduire, rire, naître, s&#39;asseoir.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "html": "<strong>Verbs that take être in the passé composé</strong> — remember <strong>DR &amp; MRS VANDERTRAMPP</strong>: <strong>D</strong>evenir, <strong>R</strong>evenir, <strong>M</strong>onter, <strong>R</strong>ester, <strong>S</strong>ortir, <strong>V</strong>enir, <strong>A</strong>ller, <strong>N</strong>aître, <strong>D</strong>escendre, <strong>E</strong>ntrer, <strong>R</strong>entrer, <strong>T</strong>omber, <strong>R</strong>etourner, <strong>A</strong>rriver, <strong>M</strong>ourir, <strong>P</strong>artir, <strong>P</strong>asser.<ul><li>In this guide: rester, arriver, sortir, entrer, devenir, revenir, tomber, monter, rentrer, mourir, naître, descendre (<em>je suis resté(e)</em>, <em>elle est née</em>).</li><li><strong>All pronominal verbs</strong> also use être: <em>je me suis levé(e)</em>, <em>je me suis assis(e)</em>, <em>je me suis souvenu(e)</em>, <em>nous nous sommes promené(e)s</em>.</li><li>With être, the past participle agrees with the subject: <em>elle est arrivée</em>, <em>ils sont sortis</em>.</li><li>Monter, descendre, sortir, rentrer and passer use <strong>avoir</strong> when they have a direct object: <em>j&#39;ai monté les valises</em> (I carried the suitcases up).</li></ul>"
    }
  ],
